Q: Compile binding for an ordinary content page A: <p>Try this...</p>
<p>This is a straightforward fix. Since the page sets <code>BindingContext = this</code>, the binding context <strong>is the page class itself</strong> — so you simply point <code>x:DataType</code> at the page's own type.</p>
<h2>The Solution</h2>
<p>Add the <code>x:DataType</code> attribute to your <code>&lt;ContentPage&gt;</code> root element, referencing your own page class. First, declare a local namespace alias, then use it:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;ContentPage xmlns=&quot;http://schemas.microsoft.com/dotnet/2021/maui&quot;
             xmlns:x=&quot;http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2009/xaml&quot;
             xmlns:local=&quot;clr-namespace:SleepDiary&quot;
             x:Class=&quot;SleepDiary.EditPage&quot;
             x:DataType=&quot;local:EditPage&quot;
             Title=&quot;EditPage&quot;&gt;
    &lt;VerticalStackLayout&gt;
        &lt;Button
            x:Name=&quot;SaveAndCloseButton&quot;
            Text=&quot;Save and close&quot;
            BackgroundColor=&quot;{Binding backgroundcolor}&quot;
            Clicked=&quot;SaveAndClose&quot;/&gt;
    &lt;/VerticalStackLayout&gt;
&lt;/ContentPage&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>The key addition is <code>xmlns:local=&quot;clr-namespace:SleepDiary&quot;</code> (replace <code>SleepDiary</code> with your actual project namespace) and <code>x:DataType=&quot;local:EditPage&quot;</code> on the root element. This tells the XAML compiler that anything bound in this page's hierarchy targets the <code>EditPage</code> class, which is exactly what <code>BindingContext = this</code> sets at runtime.</p>
<h2>Why This Works</h2>
<p><code>x:DataType</code> does <strong>not</strong> require a ViewModel — it just needs to match whatever the <code>BindingContext</code> actually is at runtime. Since you're setting <code>BindingContext = this</code> (i.e., the page itself), your page class <em>is</em> the view model in this pattern. The compiler will then verify at build time that <code>backgroundcolor</code> is a valid property on <code>EditPage</code>, eliminating the warning and giving you a small performance boost.</p>
<h2>One Optional Cleanup</h2>
<p>The <code>BindableProperty</code> field in your code-behind should be <code>static readonly</code> (not just <code>private readonly</code>) for <code>BindableProperty</code> to function correctly as designed in MAUI:</p>
<pre><code>private static readonly BindableProperty bBackgroundcolor =
    BindableProperty.Create(nameof(backgroundcolor), typeof(Color), typeof(EditPage));
</code></pre>
<p>This is a common gotcha — instance-level <code>BindableProperty</code> fields don't behave correctly because the property system expects them to be shared across all instances.</p>

