Q: Failed to execute &#39;appendBuffer&#39; on &#39;SourceBuffer&#39;: The HTMLMediaElement.error attribute is not null A: <p>This is could be happen when source buffer still updating and you call appendBuffer to append another buffer to it. You should wait the updateend event called then you're allowed to append another buffer. You can use Promise to have an await function waiting until it resolve then you append another buffer like this:</p>
<pre><code>const sourceBuffer = mediaSource.addSourceBuffer(mimeType);

const loadBuffer = (buffer) =&gt; {
  return new Promise((resolve)=&gt;{
    sourceBuffer.addEvenListener('updateend',() resolve);
    sourceBuffer.append(buffer);
  });
};

await loadBuffer(buffer1);
await loadBuffer(buffer2);

</code></pre>

Q: How to prevent Entity Framework Core from using Table-Per-Hierarchy (TPH) mapping A: <p>You can do it by introducing an intermediary base class for the bodies you want to use TPH while reserving the main base class for either TPH/TPT inheritance. For instance I introduce a &quot;SpaceBody&quot; class for the items that will use a TPH discriminator:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>    public abstract class SpaceObject
    {
        [Key, DatabaseGenerated(DatabaseGeneratedOption.Identity)]
        public int Id { get; set;  }
        public string Name { get; set; }
    }

    [Table(&quot;SpaceObjects&quot;)]
    public abstract class SpaceBody : SpaceObject
    {
    }

    public class Planet : SpaceBody
    {
        public const string Discriminator = &quot;planet&quot;;
    }

    public class Asteroid : SpaceBody
    {
        public const string Discriminator = &quot;rock&quot;;
    }

    [Table(&quot;Pods&quot;)]
    public class Pod : SpaceObject
    {
        public string Pilot { get; set; }
    }
</code></pre>
<p>Here I point SpaceBody at the SpaceObjects table, though you could call the table &quot;SpaceBodies&quot; to be less potentially confusing or use a different name for the lowest base class, and use SpaceObject for the TPH mapping sub-class.</p>
<p>Then in configuration:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>            modelBuilder.Entity&lt;SpaceBody&gt;()
                .HasDiscriminator&lt;string&gt;(&quot;Discriminator&quot;)
                .HasValue&lt;Planet&gt;(Planet.Discriminator)
                .HasValue&lt;Asteroid&gt;(Asteroid.Discriminator);

            modelBuilder.Entity&lt;Pod&gt;();
</code></pre>
<p>The DbContext can have a <code>DbSet&lt;SpaceBody&gt;</code> and <code>DbSet&lt;Pod&gt;</code> then read/write bodies and pods.</p>
<p>Test:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>            using var context = new TestDbContext();

            var planets = new[] {
                new Planet { Name = &quot;Earth&quot; },
                new Planet{Name = &quot;Mars&quot;}
            };

            var asteroids = new[] {
                new Asteroid { Name = &quot;X-31&quot; },
                new Asteroid{Name = &quot;XQ-101&quot;}
            };

            var pod = new Pod { Name = &quot;Enterprise&quot;, Pilot = &quot;Kirk&quot; };

            foreach(var  planet in planets)
                context.SpaceBodies.Add(planet);
            foreach(var asteroid in asteroids)
                context.SpaceBodies.Add(asteroid);
            context.Pods.Add(pod);
            context.SaveChanges();

            var testPlanets = context.SpaceBodies.OfType&lt;Planet&gt;().ToList();
            var testPod = context.Pods.FirstOrDefault(x =&gt; x.Pilot == &quot;Kirk&quot;);
</code></pre>
<p>This does require the Pods table to conform to the same shared column names used in the base class and TPH table. However, if you do want to use a different column name, for instance Pods table to use &quot;PodName&quot; instead of &quot;Name&quot; then you can introduce the mapping change in the configuration:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>            modelBuilder.Entity&lt;Pod&gt;()
                .Property(x =&gt; x.Name)
                .HasColumnName(&quot;PodName&quot;);
</code></pre>
